00:00:00.580 --> 00:00:03.760
Leah: So, what do we mean when
we talk about security by design?

00:00:04.670 --> 00:00:11.280
Security has often long been considered an afterthought in
the software development process, with measures to address

00:00:11.300 --> 00:00:14.710
potential issues, typically tacked on just before release.

00:00:15.799 --> 00:00:22.959
This can cause serious issues as components are
so interdependent and changing a single piece of

00:00:22.960 --> 00:00:26.020
code could affect the whole application adversely.

00:00:26.550 --> 00:00:32.820
Chris: The focus previously has on implementing
security controls around physical infrastructure such as

00:00:32.820 --> 00:00:40.720
networks, servers and endpoints using firewalls, but this
approach, is no longer adequate in sustaining today's

00:00:40.780 --> 00:00:44.650
expectations for rapid and reliable deployment of software.

00:00:45.389 --> 00:00:51.070
Leah: In software development you will hear all about
the ‘software development lifecycle’, but you will

00:00:51.110 --> 00:00:54.190
also encounter the ‘security by design lifecycle’.

00:00:54.699 --> 00:01:01.589
Both of these terms are closely linked as the starting point
for both is the initial idea, which is then taken through

00:01:01.590 --> 00:01:05.870
the stages of design, develop, test, deliver and support.

00:01:06.450 --> 00:01:11.790
Chris: The difference between the two is that ‘Security
by design’ focuses on the requirements relating to

00:01:11.790 --> 00:01:20.249
relevant security features such as data integrity,
authentication, confidentiality, authorisation and privacy.

00:01:20.910 --> 00:01:26.660
Leah: Taking ‘security by design’ into consideration
at the very beginning of a project helps to

00:01:26.900 --> 00:01:32.410
reduce costs and reduce the potential risks
that may be faced when the software is released.

00:01:33.220 --> 00:01:39.000
Building security into the development process
and testing cycles, is good practice and can

00:01:39.000 --> 00:01:42.049
save on costs, extra time and reliability.

00:01:42.539 --> 00:01:47.060
This will in turn also help to maintain
client confidence in their product.

00:01:47.590 --> 00:01:53.700
Chris: ‘Security by design’ is about having a risk-based
approach to development that focuses on continuous

00:01:53.790 --> 00:02:00.840
assessment, analysis, improvement, and validation
to create safer practices and better safety nets.

00:02:01.799 --> 00:02:07.060
Leah: An analogy that is often used in security
is the ‘Castle and Moat model’ that means

00:02:07.100 --> 00:02:09.290
everyone inside the castle is protected.

00:02:09.669 --> 00:02:14.890
Think of the moat and the drawbridge as
high-quality code that create that security and

00:02:14.890 --> 00:02:17.429
the tack on elements are like the foot soldiers.

00:02:18.090 --> 00:02:23.490
If the castle walls and the moat and drawbridge
are in good order, then the foot soldiers will only

00:02:23.490 --> 00:02:26.340
be needed in emergencies or in case of a breach.

00:02:26.870 --> 00:02:34.120
But failure to create a secure structure in the first place
and maintain it well, will result in cracks appearing which

00:02:34.170 --> 00:02:37.270
even though they can be filled, will eventually give way!

00:02:38.080 --> 00:02:41.471
Chris: Failure to incorporate security by design will
continue the cycle of further development, patches and

00:02:41.471 --> 00:02:41.479
bug fixes as and when required which takes up further
time, resources and for those who are using the software

00:02:41.479 --> 00:02:41.504
a potential lack of confidence in the software itself.

